WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
We saw water spread into every compartment, so plasma sodium concentration reports

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.100
water. Potassium breaks that pattern.

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.240
Almost all of it sits inside cells.

4
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.700
So what guards the little that is left outside?

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.040
About 98 percent of the body's potassium is inside cells, mostly skeletal muscle.

6
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.540
Outside, 14 litres hold about 4.2 mEq per litre.

7
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:37.260
That is about 59, roughly 1.7 percent of the body's 3,500.

8
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:44.090
A day's intake is about 70 mEq, more than the whole pool outside the cells.

9
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.370
So the first defence is the muscle cell, not the kidney.

10
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:54.573
Insulin and epinephrine drive potassium into muscle by stimulating the

11
00:00:54.573 --> 00:00:56.013
sodium-potassium pump.

12
00:00:57.213 --> 00:00:58.953
Insulin acts through its receptor.

13
00:01:00.353 --> 00:01:03.253
Epinephrine acts through beta2-adrenergic receptors.

14
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:05.913
This shift works in minutes.

15
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.433
Here is the one idea.

16
00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:13.573
Potassium is defended first by shifting into cells, in minutes.

17
00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:16.773
Then the kidney secretes it, over hours.

18
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:20.917
The kidney's arm is secretion, not filtration.

19
00:01:22.377 --> 00:01:26.617
Glomeruli filter about 756 mEq a day.

20
00:01:27.877 --> 00:01:30.617
The proximal tubule and loop take most of it back.

21
00:01:31.937 --> 00:01:36.837
Less than 10 percent reaches the distal nephron, a fraction that changes little with

22
00:01:36.837 --> 00:01:37.837
diet.

23
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.970
Yet urinary potassium follows intake.

24
00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.030
So the part that rises with intake is secreted.

25
00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.690
Principal cells release potassium into the tubule through ROMK channels.

26
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:56.510
At high flow, BK channels add more, much of that in intercalated cells.

27
00:01:57.583 --> 00:02:03.483
Two major influences set the rate: aldosterone-type activity, and the sodium and

28
00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:05.083
water delivered to these cells.

29
00:02:06.223 --> 00:02:07.823
Higher flow raises secretion.

30
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:14.873
Aldosterone runs high in two states: after a volume loss, and when plasma potassium

31
00:02:14.873 --> 00:02:18.953
is high. In which does the kidney secrete more potassium?

32
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.760
When potassium is high.

33
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.000
Delivery of sodium and water is normal or higher.

34
00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.000
Angiotensin II has not risen, and potassium itself stimulates secretion.

35
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:44.020
After a volume loss, delivery is low and angiotensin II is high, which restrains

36
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.020
secretion.

37
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.560
Aldosterone is high in both.

38
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.960
What differs is what comes with it.

39
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:55.820
One hormone, two outcomes: this is the aldosterone paradox.

40
00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:00.350
So potassium has two lines of defence on two clocks.

41
00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.790
Within minutes, muscle takes up the load.

42
00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:10.470
Over hours, the kidney secretes it, steered by aldosterone and by sodium and water

43
00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:10.810
delivery.
